  "narrative": "Brightline Retail, an Austin DTC home-goods seller, requires a buyer to confirm every order its replenishment agent builds. On 2026-08-14 the confirmation screen showed a $52.40 single-sample order from Kiln & Co. The mandate that was actually signed covers $1,292.20 of full case-pack inventory. Every other check passes: the credential verifies, the chain hashes recompute, the merchant is approved, the MCC is in scope, the amount is under the $1,500 cap and the month is well under its ceiling. Nothing in the signed record describes the screen, so the divergence is invisible to everything except a direct comparison of consent_ceremony.rendered_values.amount against cart_total. This is F29, and it is the case that best explains why cryptographic validity is not legitimacy.",
